  "strategy": "**1. Approach and Rationale**  \nThe central idea is to use **multi-resolution neuroevolution** combined with a novel **wavelet-smoothed crossover** to escape local optima in high-dimensional nonconvex optimization. Unlike gradient-based methods, this approach explores the solution space using probabilistic neural networks (PNN) that parameterize step functions. By gradually increasing resolution and injecting neural-driven mutations, we achieve better exploration of the optimal frontier. The wavelet-smoothing ensures spatial regularity, mimicking biological sensory adaptation, reducing the disregard for boundary terms observed in direct correlation methods.  \n\n**2. Mathematical Specification**  \n- **Representation**: A step function \\( h: [0,2] \\to [0,1] \\) is represented as a PNN with:  \n  \\(\\theta = \\{\\mathbf{W} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{L \\times D}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^L, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^+\\}\\)  \n  where \\( D = n_{\\text{points}}} \\) (resolution), \\( \\mathbf{W} \\) and \\( \\mathbf{b} \\) define activation functions, and \\( \\mathbf{v} \\) controls the intensity of nonlinearity.  \n- **Objective**: Minimize \\( \\max_{k} \\left( \\frac{1}{\\Delta x} \\iint h(x)(1-h(x+k)) \\, dx  \\right) \\), approximated by:  \n  \\( C_5 = \\Delta x \\cdot \\max(\\operatorname{corr}(h, 1-h)) \\), where \\( \\Delta x = 2/n_\\text{points} \\).  \n- **Constraints**: \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\), \\( \\sum h[i] \\Delta x = 1 \\).  \n- **Neuroevolution**: The PNN is optimized via covariance matrix adaptation evolution strategy (CMA-ES), with wavelet-smoothed mutations.  \n\n**3. Implementation Procedure**  \n- **Step 1**: Sample PNN parameters \\( \\theta \\) from a Gaussian process prior.  \n- **Step 2**: Convert \\( \\theta \\) to \\( h \\) via:  \n  \\( h = \\sigma(\\mathbf{W} \\cdot \\Sigma) + \\text{clip}(0,1) \\), with \\( \\Sigma \\) a covariance embedding.  \n- **Step 3**: Enforce constraints using iterative clipping and sum adjustment.  \n- **Step 4**: Evaluate \\( C_5 \\) via full correlation and report the maximum.  \n- **Step 5**: **Multi-resolution roll-by-roll GA**: Start at \\( n_\\text{points} = 128 \\), then double resolution:  \n  - At each level, use CMA-ES to optimize the PNN \\( \\theta \\) for 500 generations.  \n  - Inject mutations via neural transfer (copy architecture from best parent, perturb \\( \\mathbf{v} \\)), followed by wavelet-smoothing to maintain continuity.  \n\n**4. Search Choices and Variations**  \n- **Starting Resolution**: 128 points (matches parent).  \n- **CMA-ES Parameters**: \\( \\sigma = 0.5\\mathbf{v} \\), mutation factor \\( \\mu = 0.5/d \\).  \n- **Neural Xover**: Copy 70% of weights from the best parent to child, perturb the rest with \\( \\mathbf{v} \\).  \n- **Wavelet Mutation**: Apply a 5-scale wavelet shrinkage with \\( \\lambda = 0.1\\sigma \\) to avoid discontinuities (e.g., ringing artifacts).  \n- **Early Termination**: Stop if \\( C_5 < 0.380 \\) or plateau detected.  \n\n**5. Compute Budget and Robustness**  \n- **Dominant Cost**: \\( O(d \\cdot L^2) \\) per CMA-ES generation, where \\( d \\) is dimension (128, 256,...), limiting resolutions to 1024 points due to 1300 seconds budget.  \n- **Scheduling**: Caution time limits per resolution (\u224810s per CMA-ES generation at 256 points). Use wavelet smoothing to reduce refinement iterations.  \n\n**6. Validation and Return Contract**  \n- **Feasibility**: Enforce \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) via iterative clipping and \\( \\sum h[i] = n_\\text{points}/2 \\).  \n- **Objective Check**: Recompute \\( C_5 \\) on final candidate.  \n- **Transfer Learning**: At resolution boundaries, interpolate best parent vector with cubic splines, then project sum.  \n\nEach resolution level is retained if \\( C_5 \\) improves by <0.1%. Results are compared strictly by raw \\( C_5 \\) value.\nEach resolution level is retained if \\( C_5 \\) improves by <0.1%. Results are compared strictly by raw \\( C_5 \\) value.",
